1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:07,311
In the last episode we laid out the grid.

2
00:00:07,311 --> 00:00:10,099
Now we place the first racks —

3
00:00:10,099 --> 00:00:14,653
and the way we line them up decides how well this

4
00:00:14,653 --> 00:00:18,929
room can be cooled for the next fifteen years.

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,291
Almost all IT equipment breathes the same way:

6
00:00:24,291 --> 00:00:28,165
it draws cool air in at the front and blows hot

7
00:00:28,165 --> 00:00:29,813
air out at the back.

8
00:00:29,813 --> 00:00:35,500
Every layout decision in the whitespace starts from that simple fact.

9
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:42,140
Line your rows up like a classroom,

10
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:44,660
all facing the same direction,

11
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:49,112
and the hot exhaust of one row becomes the intake air

12
00:00:49,112 --> 00:00:53,648
of the next. Each row runs warmer than the one before.

13
00:00:53,648 --> 00:00:58,268
You end up over-cooling the whole room just to keep the

14
00:00:58,268 --> 00:01:00,200
last row within limits.

15
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:07,359
The solution is to alternate.

16
00:01:07,359 --> 00:01:10,040
Rows face each other front-to-front,

17
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:11,678
creating a cold aisle,

18
00:01:11,678 --> 00:01:14,582
and back-to-back, creating a hot aisle.

19
00:01:14,582 --> 00:01:18,678
Cold air is delivered only where equipment takes it in,

20
00:01:18,678 --> 00:01:21,954
and hot air is collected where it comes out,

21
00:01:21,954 --> 00:01:24,486
and returned to the cooling units.

22
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:34,302
Now align the rows to the floor grid.

23
00:01:34,302 --> 00:01:37,405
Place the rack fronts on a tile edge,

24
00:01:37,405 --> 00:01:40,843
so the cold aisle consists of full tiles.

25
00:01:40,843 --> 00:01:45,958
That way every perforated tile can be placed exactly where it

26
00:01:45,958 --> 00:01:50,486
is needed — and moved later without cutting the floor.

27
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:56,216
Before you fill the room,

28
00:01:56,216 --> 00:01:58,635
reserve the space people need.

29
00:01:58,635 --> 00:02:03,070
A front clearance of at least one point two metres lets

30
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:05,247
you install deep equipment;

31
00:02:05,247 --> 00:02:09,440
at the rear, keep at least one metre for cabling and

32
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:13,875
service. Leave passages at the ends of rows for egress,

33
00:02:13,875 --> 00:02:17,343
and check them against the local fire code.

34
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:23,843
Three mistakes we see on site:

35
00:02:23,843 --> 00:02:28,952
one rack turned the wrong way in an otherwise correct row;

36
00:02:28,952 --> 00:02:32,739
an open gap in a row where hot air can wrap

37
00:02:32,739 --> 00:02:37,408
around; and perforated tiles placed in the hot aisle.

38
00:02:37,408 --> 00:02:40,315
Each one mixes hot and cold air —

39
00:02:40,315 --> 00:02:43,486
and wastes the cooling you paid for.

40
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:50,099
On site, check four things:

41
00:02:50,099 --> 00:02:52,739
all fronts face the cold aisle;

42
00:02:52,739 --> 00:02:55,208
rows are aligned to the grid;

43
00:02:55,208 --> 00:02:57,847
clearances are free and marked;

44
00:02:57,847 --> 00:03:00,657
and every gap in a row is closed.

45
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:08,831
To recap: alternate your rows into hot and cold aisles.

46
00:03:08,831 --> 00:03:11,062
Align them to the floor grid.

47
00:03:11,062 --> 00:03:13,600
And plan clearances before racks.

48
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,846
Next: zones and functions —

49
00:03:19,846 --> 00:03:23,929
where the MDA, HDA and EDA belong in this room.
